<Layout title="About" description="Meet Wharton Chan: Hong Kong-born, Singapore-based junior doctor, researcher, jazz saxophonist and clarinetist, and educator trained at Oxford and Duke-NUS.">
  <PageIntro eyebrow="A little about Wharton" title="Different disciplines.<br />The same curiosity." description="Medicine, music, and education all begin with listening carefully. What follows is a question, a pattern, a response — and, hopefully, a connection." />
  <div class="container"><section class="split-content"><div class="art-placeholder" role="img" aria-label="Portrait placeholder for Wharton Chan"><strong>W.</strong><span>Wharton Chan</span><small>Professional portrait to be supplied</small></div><div><h2>Listening for what matters.</h2><p style="margin-top:25px">Born and raised in Hong Kong, Wharton now works in Singapore as a junior doctor and researcher. He studied Molecular and Cellular Biochemistry at the University of Oxford before completing an MD–PhD at Duke-NUS Medical School.</p><p>In science, he is drawn to evidence and the questions it raises. In the clinic, careful listening and thoughtful responses matter. On the bandstand, the same attentiveness makes room for a conversation in sound.</p><p>Wharton plays alto saxophone and clarinet as a frontline musician with The Marmalade Jammers. Lindy Hop helped him rediscover jazz, and the connection between music and movement remains central to his playing.</p><p>He also enjoys teaching and helping students discover their own paths. Across medicine, research, performance, and mentorship, he values curiosity, evidence, trust, integrity, and human connection.</p><a class="text-link" href="/contact/">Continue the conversation <svg class="arrow" viewBox="0 0 16 16" fill="none" aria-hidden="true"><path d="M3 13L13 3M3 3H13V13" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="1.2" /></svg></a></div></section><section class="quote-section"><div class="eyebrow">A guiding philosophy</div><blockquote>“To touch the hearts of many,<br />to heal some, and to cure a few.”</blockquote></section></div>
